<?nvs
// The two stores, as a program: the tier that may forget, the tier that may
// not, and the limit built on the second one.
//
// [ADR 0059](/docs/decisions/0059.md) § 1 makes
// these **two methods rather than one API with a flag**, so which
// guarantee a program relies on is visible in review rather than hidden in
// an argument. `local()` is per-core and in-process, and its contract says
// any entry may be absent at any time, for any reason — the first two lines
// below are the only order in which that tier can be asserted at all, and a
// program that would be wrong if a `get` returned nothing is using the
// wrong one. `shared()` is a real store over the network, coherent across
// cores and machines, and gated by `cache.shared` — the grant names the store
// an operator configured, not the address it happens to be at.
//
// Values cross by the recursive graph copy the isolate boundary already
// uses (ADR 0023), not by a third mechanism: `put` copies out of the
// request heap, `get` copies back into it, because the request heap is
// dropped wholesale at the end of the request.
//
// [ADR 0075](/docs/decisions/0075.md) is the last two lines.
// `Core\RateLimit` takes **no configuration at all** — the limit is an
// argument because only the application knows whether this is a quota or a
// lock — and an unreachable store **throws** rather than deciding
// *allowed*. The `retryAfter` on a refusal is what makes a `429` honest.
//
// The five lines this prints are frozen by an exact check in a goal record
// under `data/goals/`; the source is not.

var $local = Core\Cache::local();

// Cold, on this core, in this process. Nothing has written it yet.
var $before = $local->get('greeting');
if ($before == null) {
    echo 'local miss', "\n";
} else {
    echo 'local hit before any write', "\n";
}

// Warm, immediately after our own write — which is the strongest thing the
// local tier promises and the only one worth asserting.
$local->put('greeting', 'hello');
var $after = $local->get('greeting');
if ($after == null) {
    echo 'local miss after a write', "\n";
} else {
    echo 'local hit', "\n";
}

// The other tier, over the network, and coherent.
var $shared = Core\Cache::shared();
$shared->put('greeting', 'hello');
var $seen = $shared->get('greeting');
if ($seen == null) {
    echo 'shared miss', "\n";
} else {
    echo 'shared hit', "\n";
}

// An account nothing has charged yet — and the reason it is drawn rather than
// written down is the tier itself. The shared store is coherent and it does not
// forget when this process exits, so a constant key would inherit the previous
// run's arrival and refuse the line below; the acceptance sweep runs this
// program three times in a few seconds. That is the whole difference from the
// tier above, stated as a line that would fail without it.
var $account = 'account:' . Core\Random::token(6);

// One request per two seconds, for this key. The first is inside the limit.
var $first = Core\RateLimit::consume($account, 1, 2s);
if ($first->allowed()) {
    echo 'allowed=true', "\n";
} else {
    echo 'allowed=false', "\n";
}

// The second is not, and the decision carries the wait rather than leaving
// the caller to invent one.
var $second = Core\RateLimit::consume($account, 1, 2s);
if ($second->allowed()) {
    echo 'allowed=true unexpectedly', "\n";
} else {
    // Rounded **up**, because a `Retry-After` rounded down tells the client to
    // come back before the store will admit it — and the wait here is a whisker
    // under the two seconds the limit is, since the second call arrives a
    // moment after the first.
    var $wait = $second->retryAfter();
    var $seconds = 0;
    if ($wait != null) {
        $seconds = $wait->toSeconds();
        if ($wait->toMilliseconds() % 1000 != 0) {
            $seconds = $seconds + 1;
        }
    }
    echo 'allowed=false retryAfter=', $seconds, "\n";
}
